{
  "dom_id": "step-q119341",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "The number of sales $S(t)$ of a new tech device $t$ weeks after the start of sales can be modeled by the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-0.1S.\n$$\n\nIf there were $100\\,000$ sales initially, calculate the number of sales after $10$ weeks. Round your answer to the nearest integer.",
  "worked_solution": "We model the number of sales using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-0.1S.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.1t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $100\\,000$  sales initially, so we have ${S}_{0}=100\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of sales $S$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nS(t)=100\\,000\\,{e}^{-0.1t}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of sales when $t=10,$ we substitute $t=10$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(10) & =100\\,000\\,{e}^{-0.1\\cdot 10} \\\\  & =100\\,000\\,{e}^{-1} \\\\  & \u224836\\,787.94\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that there are $36\\,788$ sales after $10$ weeks.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
